<div class="hero"><div class="wrap">
  <div class="kicker">The detection core</div>
  <h1>Molecular identification for gas chromatography</h1>
  <p class="lede">A detector that tells you <em>what</em> eluted, not just that something did — reading each compound&rsquo;s molecular spectrum as it leaves the column. No vacuum system. No ion source. This is the core technology beneath every AATG instrument.</p>
</div></div>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <h2>Knowing that something eluted is not knowing what it is</h2>
  <p>Gas chromatography separates a mixture reliably. Identifying what came out is the harder half, and most detectors cannot do it. Flame ionisation, photoionisation and electron capture report that a compound passed and when — identity is inferred from retention time alone.</p>
  <p>Mass spectrometry identifies properly, and it brings vacuum hardware, a service contract, a trained operator and, usually, a core facility. That cost is why so much volatile analysis still runs on retention time and a best guess.</p>
</div></section>

<section class="alt"><div class="wrap">
  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <div class="flow">
    <div class="step"><span class="n">1</span><b>Sample</b><span>headspace, thermal desorption, breath</span></div>
    <div class="step"><span class="n">2</span><b>Separation</b><span>fast GC column</span></div>
    <div class="step"><span class="n">3</span><b>Detection</b><span>Raman spectrum, collected in flow</span></div>
    <div class="step"><span class="n">4</span><b>Identification</b><span>compound, amount, confidence</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>A spectrum for every peak</h3><p>Each compound is identified from its own molecular fingerprint rather than from where it happens to land in the chromatogram.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Partial separation is enough</h3><p>Because each peak carries a spectrum as well as a retention time, compounds that overlap in time can still be told apart. The separation does not have to be perfect.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Confidence on every call</h3><p>Results come with a calibrated confidence, so a non-specialist knows when to trust an assignment and when to send the sample out.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>No vacuum, no ion source</h3><p>Nothing to pump down, no turbo to rebuild, no consumable ion source, and no mass spectrometrist required to keep it running.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section><div class="wrap">
  <h2>Where it applies</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card"><h3>Terpene and flavour profiling</h3><p>Positional isomers such as alpha- and beta-pinene sit close on a standard column and are poorly distinguished by electron-impact mass spectra. Their Raman spectra differ.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Environmental volatiles</h3><p>On-site identification of volatile contaminants without the infrastructure a mass spectrometer needs.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Breath and biomarker research</h3><p>The analytical core beneath <a href="index.html">BreathDX</a>, our point-of-care breath screening programme.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Bioprocess and quality control</h3><p>Formulations and raw materials at volumes where mass spectrometry is too slow per sample.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<section class="alt"><div class="wrap">
  <h2>Where the work stands</h2>
  <div class="status">
    <p>The subsystems are built and running independently: fast gas chromatography column modules, the Raman detection system on a sub-micron positioning stage, and the inference layer that turns a spectrum and a retention time into an identified compound.</p>
    <p>Integration is the current work. The U.S. National Science Foundation has invited AATG to submit a full SBIR Phase I proposal under its Scientific Instrumentation topic.</p>
    <p class="note" style="margin-bottom:0;">Performance targets quoted elsewhere on this site are design targets for the intended product, not measured results. We would rather say so.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:22px;"><b>Looking further out.</b> Chemical-threat and explosive trace detection &mdash; the application area behind <a href="r-guard.html">r-Guard</a> and r-Trace &mdash; needs a shorter excitation wavelength than the present configuration uses, to reach the spectral region where those compounds show their strongest bands. That is a later stage of the same platform rather than a current capability.</p>
</div></section>

